  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Monday...\n\nWith the region transitioning into a more southwesterly flow,\nseveral shortwave disturbances are set to bring daily chances of\nisolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms. For today, low\nCAPE and shear values give low confidence in any strong \nthunderstorms. Any thunderstorms look to remain over the \nmountains and foothills west and southwest of Billings with \nBillings at about a 15% chance for a shower and weak thunderstorm.\nA few rumbles of thunder are also possible (15% chance) across \nsoutheast MT during the early morning hours as that same wave \npushes through. Into Wednesday, a wave moving up through central \nMT looks to bring several rounds of scattered showers and \nthunderstorms as depicted by hi-res models. MUCAPE values look \nmodest with values of up to 750 J/kg and around 25-30 kts of shear\nfor areas west of Rosebud county. With this, a few strong to \nsevere storms are possible across central MT with the main threat \nbeing strong winds. Additionally, wildfire smoke from wildfires in\nnorthwest Canada and the southwest US will continue to bring in \nsmoke with most of it staying in the upper levels of the \natmosphere. Into Thursday and Friday, the threat for severe \nweather looks to shift to southeast MT when ensembles show a wave \npassing through northeast WY and the Dakotas both days. Ensembles \nare showing good signals for instability, so this will be \nsomething to watch. Into the weekend, ridging begins to build in \noverhead with ensembles depicting some weak energy moving through \nsoutheast MT Saturday evening, leading to small precipitation \nchances (10-20%). Ensembles and clusters are in good agreement \nfor ridging to strengthen into next week, ushering in warmer and \ndryer conditions. \n\nHigh temperatures through the period will be in the upper 70s and\n80s, reaching the low 90s by next week. TS",
